## Security

- Accounts: e-mail + password stored as salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (600,000 iterations);
  sign-in does the same hashing work for unknown e-mails. Failed sign-ins are counted in
  sliding 15-minute windows: per e-mail and client address at most 5 (the "rate limit on
  sign-in (5 failures per 15 minutes)"), per client address for any e-mails at most 20,
  and per e-mail from all addresses together at most 20. A full window refuses sign-in
  (429 with `Retry-After`, before any password is checked or hashed) until the oldest
  failure in it is 15 minutes old. So rotating e-mails cannot make one client burn
  unbounded CPU and one address cannot lock an account out for another address; 20
  failures from several addresses together hold that e-mail everywhere except at
  addresses that signed in to it successfully in the last 30 days. The counters are in
  memory and reset on restart; behind a reverse proxy every client shares the proxy's
  address, so these limits then apply to all clients together: rate-limit at the proxy by
  the real client address too. Sessions are random tokens (only their SHA-256 is stored) in an
  `HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict` cookie that expires after 12 hours.
- Every API route except `/api/health` and `/api/login` needs a session and the role
  permission for its method. Roles: @@ROLES@@.
- CSRF: every write needs the session's `X-CSRF-Token`, `Content-Type: application/json`
  and a same-origin request (`Origin`/`Sec-Fetch-Site` checked); no CORS headers are sent.
- Input validation for every field (type, required, length, value range, enumeration,
  strict dates and e-mail), unknown and read-only fields refused, body size limit
  (@@MAX_BODY@@ bytes), duplicate JSON keys refused; parameterized SQL only.
- Headers on every response: `Content-Security-Policy` (`default-src 'self'`,
  `frame-ancestors 'none'`, no inline code), `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
  `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, COOP/CORP.
- No secrets in the repository, no shell, no `eval`; the frontend never uses `innerHTML`.
- The server refuses non-loopback binding unless `--allow-network` is given; deploy it only
  behind TLS (with `--secure-cookies`) and a reverse proxy (open item OI-DEPLOY).

